  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 429 PM CDT Sat Sep 5 2026\n\n...Synopsis...\n\nUpper ridging continues to build over the Eastern Plains, with \nseveral weak shortwaves evident on water vapor. A couple such \nshortwaves have been producing showers and storms over northwestern \nMN and along the north central ND/Manitoba border.\nThe next more significant shortwave is still down over WY and will \nmove into the Northern Plains tonight into tomorrow morning. The \nupper ridge will begin to shift slightly east Sunday night, leaving \nthe region in southwesterly flow aloft into Labor Day. A broad \ntrough/upper low will move through south central Canada on Tuesday, \nleaving the Northern Plains in slightly northwesterly to near zonal \nflow aloft through the rest of the work week and into next Saturday.\n\n...Marginal severe chances tonight, tomorrow and Monday...\n\nSome MU CAPE still around, but SPC mesoanalysis page has a lot of \nCIN with the mixed layer CAPE. Most of the CAMs don't have much over \neastern ND until later tonight when the shortwave over WY comes \nnortheast, but given recent poor performance do not have much trust \nin their solutions. There will be plenty of MU CAPE again overnight, \nalthough bulk shear will be fairly modest around 30 kts. Some \nconvection could be ongoing tomorrow morning, with HREF showing a \nlow probability of strong updraft helicity tracks in west central \nMN. Another shortwave coming through could bring more thunderstorm \nactivity Sunday afternoon and evening, although much will depend on \nhow the morning activity plays out and if the atmosphere recovers. \nBetter shear looks to be in store for Monday, although instability \nis more uncertain. At this point, can't rule out an isolated \ninstance of hail up to ping pong balls or 60 mph winds through the \nrest of the holiday weekend, but confidence in exactly when and \nwhere is low.",
